        <title xml:id="name5652-2" xml:lang="syr" srophe:tags="#syriaca-headword" source="#bib5652-1">ܐܓܪܬܐ ܕܡܠܟܐ ܩܘܣܛܢܛܝܢܘܣ. ܕܩܪܝܐ ܠܐܦܝܣ̈ܩܘܦܐ ܡܢ ܐܢܩܘܪܐ ܠܢܝܩܝܐ</title>
        <title xml:id="name5652-3" xml:lang="en" source="#bib5652-2">An Epistle of Constantine the King summoning the Bishops from Ancyra to Nice</title>
        <title xml:id="name5652-4" xml:lang="syr" source="#bib5652-8">ܐܓܪܬܐ ܕܩܘܣܛܢܛܝܢܘܣ ܪܒܐ ܐܒܘܗܝ ܕܩܘܣܛܢܛܝܢܣ ܙܥܘܪܐ ܕܩܪܝܐ ܠܐ̈ܦܝܣܩܦܐ ܡܢ ܐܢܩܘܪܐ ܠܢܝܩܝܐ</title>
        <author ref="http://syriaca.org/person/1655" source="#bib5652-1 #bib5652-3" xml:lang="en">Constantine the Great — <foreign xml:lang="syr">ܩܘܣܛܢܛܝܢܘܣ</foreign></author>
        <idno type="URI">http://syriaca.org/work/5652</idno>
        <textLang mainLang="syr"/>
        <noteGrp type="abstract">
          <desc xml:lang="en">Abstract</desc>
          <note corresp="http://syriaca.org/nhsl" xml:lang="en" type="abstract" source="#bib5652-1 #bib5652-2 #bib5652-9 #bib5652-10">A letter of the emperor <persName ref="http://syriaca.org/person/1655">Constantine</persName> summoning the bishops to <placeName ref="http://syriaca.org/place/621">Nicaea</placeName> in <placeName ref="http://syriaca.org/place/5587">Bithynia</placeName> instead of <placeName ref="http://syriaca.org/place/494">Ancyra</placeName> in <placeName ref="http://syriaca.org/place/2669">Galatia</placeName>, where the synod had previously been agreed. Constantine explains that bishops from <placeName ref="http://syriaca.org/place/592">Italy</placeName> and the rest of <placeName ref="http://syriaca.org/place/591">Europe</placeName> are coming and that the air of Nicaea is temperate. He also announces that he will attend as a spectator and participant. The Greek original is lost, and the letter survives only in Syriac, among the documents on the Council of Nicaea that precede its canons in Syriac canonical collections. Cowper, who first published the text in 1857, thought it probably one of the letters to the bishops mentioned by <persName ref="http://syriaca.org/person/480">Eusebius</persName> in his <title>Life of Constantine</title>.</note>
        </noteGrp>
